\n\nEU^P(π) = E_P[V | do_G(D=π)]\nπ^* ∈ argmax_{π∈Π} EU^P(π)\nP-FDT(P,G,o) = π^*(o)\n\nIf stochastic policies: D selects distribution over actions; intervention do(D=π) where π maps histories to distributions; expected utility integrates. But source likely deterministic; we can mention stochastic extension.\n\nNeed contrast:\n\nEDT: choose a maximizing E[V | O=o, A=a].\nCDT: choose a maximizing E[V | do(A=a), O=o].\nAction-level FDT: choose a maximizing E[V | do(D_x=a), O? maybe not condition].\nP-FDT: choose π maximizing E[V | do(D=π)] and then apply o.\n\nNeed discuss observation treatment: Source action-level FDT does not respond by conditioning on observation but by intervening on observation-indexed node. For P-FDT, I propose operational interpretation: The agent uses all observations to update model P (so P is posterior) but the policy intervention is not conditioned on the actual observation when evaluating EU; rather, actual observation determines which component of π is executed. This yields \"updateless\" choice of policy relative to observation-dependent payoff. But if observations also provide information about payoff-relevant states not affected by policy, should P-FDT update? Yes: P should be Bayesian updated on observations as evidence about exogenous variables. But when evaluating policies, do not restrict expectation to worlds where actual observation occurred; instead integrate over observation variable as downstream of policy (where appropriate). However if observation is already known and cannot be changed by policy? Need subtle. In transparent Newcomb, observation full is downstream of predictor/policy; P-FDT should not condition on full when choosing policy because doing so would break dependency. But after seeing full, P is updated? The source action-level FDT doesn't condition on obs; it changes intervention node. For policy version, if after seeing full, you still choose policy. You know you saw full; but the counterfactual policy evaluation